2)Why is the 3-arg LinkedHashMap constructor used with true? What behavior changes if it’s false?
When that third argument (accessOrder) is set to true, the map automatically reorders its underlying doubly-linked list 
every time an element is interacted with:When you call .get(key) or .put(key, value): The map takes that element and 
moves it to the very end of the list (marking it as the most recently used).
The consequence: The element at the very head of the list is always the one that hasn't been touched for the longest 

time (the least recently used).
What changes if it’s false:
false means insertion-order, not access-order. Reads (get) do not move entries.
Then eviction removes the oldest inserted entry, which is FIFO-ish, not LRU.

3)Time complexity of get and put (average + worst case) and why.
Time complexity:
Average case: get O(1), put O(1) (HashMap + linked list pointer updates).
Worst case: get/put can degrade to O(n) in pathological hash-collision scenarios (n = number of entries, 
here up to cap, but generally n). In modern Java there’s also tree-binning for heavy collisions, which can make 
worst-case closer to O(log n) for those bins, but interview-safe answer is: average O(1), worst O(n).

4)Name one subtle production issue with this implementation and how you’d fix it.
Not thread-safe: concurrent access can corrupt internal state / violate LRU ordering.
Fix: wrap with synchronization (e.g., Collections.synchronizedMap) or use an explicit lock around get/put, or use Caffeine/Guava cache in real systems.
Capacity edge cases: cap <= 0 leads to surprising behavior (everything immediately evicted).
Fix: validate in constructor and throw IllegalArgumentException.
Boxing overhead (Integer keys/values) if hot path; consider primitive specialized structures if needed.
